  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/3a512ef7399ac46c611335a9f957bfae9a14e802ddd39e492f6184e0e149fb7e.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}?$",
  "worked_solution": "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{3}x,\\frac{1}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the endpoints $A(-6,-3)$ and $B(3,6),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6,-3) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3)=(-2,-1) \\\\ (3,6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6)=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting points are ${A}^{′}(-2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,2).$\n\nFinally, we connect ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ using a segment, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/ba27f325ca104397544c7b5d19078d59cf63209a83d89de73cdeb752f5c0fc84.png)",
